{{also|pullout|pull-out}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-pull out.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{&lit|en|pull|out}}
#: {{ux|en|I need to '''pull''' the splinter '''out''' of my hand.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[withdraw]]; especially of [[military]] [[forces]]; to [[retreat]].
#: {{ux|en|The troops '''pulled out''' of the conflict.}}
#: {{ux|en|The mayor '''pulled out''' of the race for Senate after numerous opinion polls had him polling at less than 10 percent.}}
#: {{ux|en|The racehorse '''pulled out''' of the Stakes with a hurt foot.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|March 11 2022|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/mar/11/chelsea-in-crisis-but-there-is-no-will-to-leave-club-on-their-knees-roman-abramovich|David Hytner|Chelsea are in crisis but there is no will to leave club on their knees|{{w|The Guardian}}
|passage=There is still time to find a buyer and for them to stabilise the operation, especially if the deal were done by 31 May – when the club’s special licence to carry on is due to expire. There remain plenty of interested parties, who can only see Chelsea’s price dropping as sponsors '''pull out''' or consider their associations; as revenue streams are hit.}}
# {{lb|en|aviation|of an aircraft}} To transition from a [[dive]] to [[level]] or [[climb]]ing [[flight]].
#: {{ux|en|After releasing its bomb, the plane '''pulled out''' of its dive.}}
# {{lb|en|literally}} To use [[coitus interruptus]] as a method of [[birth control]].
#: {{ux|en|With a tremendous groan, he '''pulled out''' and ejaculated all over her belly.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[remove]] something from a [[container]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''pulled''' his gun '''out''' before she had a chance to scream.}}
#:{{synonyms|en|whip out|draw}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[maneuver]] a vehicle from the side of a road onto the [[lane]].
#: {{ux|en|When joining a road, you should check for traffic before '''pulling out'''.}}
# To [[draw out]] or [[lengthen]].

